          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Chapter Summary"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.601,
            0.707,
            0.623
          ],
          "content": "In this chapter you have learnt the following concepts:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.63,
            0.885,
            0.693
          ],
          "content": "• **Natural Numbers** ( ) are the counting numbers {1, 2, 3, … } that emerged at least tens of thousands of years ago due to humanity’s need to count."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.7,
            0.886,
            0.825
          ],
          "content": "The **Concept of Zero (Śhūnya)** was formalised in India by philosophers and then brought into mathematics formally by Brahmagupta (629 CE), who transformed the philosophical state of ‘nothingness’ (Śhūnyatā) into an actual number on which one could perform arithmetic operations. Brahmagupta also introduced the negative numbers, i.e., numbers less than zero."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.832,
            0.887,
            0.914
          ],
          "content": "**Integers** (Z) extend the number line to the left of 1 to include zero as well as the negative numbers, which Brahmagupta historically categorised as ‘debts’ (ṛiṇa) in contrast with the positive number ‘fortunes’ (dhana)."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
